From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] context_server: a raw request that keeps the server's error,
 and its instructions
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo proposal §6.69 step 3 (ask 94) and §6.13. The patch after this one makes
Zeo answer an external agent's MCP requests from the context server Zeo
already runs (MCP-over-ACP). A relay has to hand back what the server said,
and the client had no way to: request_with parses into a typed response and
turns an error object into its message alone, so the code a caller acts on
(-32602 for bad arguments, a server's own codes) and the data were lost. And
InitializeResponse dropped the server's instructions (MCP 2025-03-26), which
the agent should still get from the server/discover answer Zeo gives in the
server's name.

- Client::request_raw / InitializedContextServerProtocol::request_raw:
  method and params as JSON, the result as JSON or the error object whole
  (McpErrorObject: code, message, data). Same timeout as request, and the
  same cancellation: a fired cancel_rx sends notifications/cancelled with the
  request's id and fails with RequestCanceled, so the caller can tell a
  cancellation from a failure. request_with now shares the send-and-wait
  half (send_request) with it; what request_with returns is unchanged.
- InitializeResponse.instructions, optional, kept when the server sends it.
- test::ScriptedTransport (test-support): answers each message with what a
  script returns -- error objects, notifications before the response, or
  nothing, to leave a request pending -- and records every message sent.
  FakeTransport can do none of the three.

Tests client_raw_tests (new): the error object survives whole; a result with
fields Zed has no type for is returned verbatim; a cancelled request tells
the server which id to drop and fails with RequestCanceled; initialize keeps
the instructions.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
